  "query_preview": "..\n\nLatest water vapor imagery clearly shows the upper level trough \nswinging through the Great Lakes region this morning, modestly \namplifying as it moves into New England today. The parent upper \nlevel low (539 DAM at 500 MB) over James Bay will slowly track east \nthrough Quebec on Friday. This results in cool to seasonable \nconditions today and Friday before a big warm up occurs on Saturday. \n\nStill looking at 850 MB temps bottoming out at 3 to 4 C tonight. \nWith the center of high pressure over the Ohio Valley, winds may \nfail to fully decouple, keeping mins elevated in the upper 30s to \nlower 40s. This is also supported by forecasted surface dew pts \nholding in that range. \n\nAn active warm front over eastern Iowa on Friday, but consensus is \nis now for the low level jet and instability axis/negative Showalter \nvalues to reside northwest of southern Lower Michigan Friday night. \nThus, dry forecast generally looks ok, but NBM pops appear to be too \nlow near zero. Trended forecast toward silent 14% pop, as the 3z \nRap/00 NAM show concern with for some potential light showers, \nespecially over Tri-Cities region.\n\nSpeaking of farther west, warm 20 C 850 MB temps are now progged to \ntrack into the Upper Peninsula late Saturday, with 17-18 C progged \nover southeast Michigan underneath a pronounced upper level ridge \naxis. With 925 MB temps still advertised at 19 to 20 C per the 00z \nNAM and surface winds out of the southwest, maxes around 80 degrees \nstill seem likely. \n\nThe forecast for the second half of the weekend into Monday will \nstill be determined by what happens to the remnants of the tropical \nsystem. Trends suggests the center will reach into the southern Ohio \nValley by Sunday evening, before be shunting off to the northeast. \nThis would lead to limited rainfall. However, the forecast can still \nchange based off the track, size, and strength of Hurricane Isaias.",
